          alt: "Underwater seal chest drainage: the water is a one-way valve — air leaves the chest, nothing gets back in. Drainage tube kept free of loops and kinks; tip 2 cm under water; air escapes; sterile water seal. What you check: swinging with breathing means the tube is patent, good; swinging has stopped means blocked, kinked or lung fully re-expanded; bubbling on coughing only is expected early; continuous bubbling is an air leak in the system; sudden heavy fresh blood — report at once. Never raise the bottle above the chest, clamp for transfer or for a walk, empty or change it casually, leave it on its side or knocked over; clamping a bubbling drain can cause tension pneumothorax. If the tube pulls out: cover with a dressing taped on three sides, not four, and call for help.",
          caption: "Swinging, bubbling, draining — three words at every hourly check, and two that are never done: lifted and clamped. The patient who walks to the toilet carries the bottle below her waist, and nobody puts a clamp on it ‘to be safe’.",
